## About this package

This package is part of the ${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_MAJOR}.${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_MINOR}.${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_PATCH} release of Emgu LiteRT. It contains:

- `Emgu.LiteRT.Models.CV.Maui.dll`: the live camera for the `${EMGU_LITERT_MODELS_CV_NUGET_ID}` detectors in a MAUI app.
  - `LiveCamera` delivers frames as Emgu CV `Mat`s (8-bit BGR, at most 960 px on the long side, turned upright as `RotationDegrees` says, up to 30 a second) and JPEG snapshots on request (`CaptureAsync`).
  - On Android it runs through Camera2, with the back or the selfie camera (`Front`). While the frames go on, it also takes full-resolution photos (`TakePhotoAsync`) and records H.264 clips with sound (`RecordClipAsync`). Where Camera2 can't be set up, it falls back to OpenCV's camera.
  - On Mac Catalyst and Windows it is OpenCV's capture of the first camera. iOS isn't supported yet (`LiveCamera.Supported`).
  - `LiveCameraPreview` shows the frames through each platform's own image view: Emgu CV's `CvImageView` on Android, a `UIImageView` on iOS and Mac Catalyst, a `WriteableBitmap` on Windows.

It depends on Emgu CV's MAUI runtime package (`Emgu.CV.runtime.maui.mini`), which brings OpenCV's native libraries for each platform.

## Getting Started

Declare camera access (`android.permission.CAMERA` in AndroidManifest.xml, `NSCameraUsageDescription` in Info.plist, the `webcam` capability on Windows) and ask for it first.

```csharp
using Emgu.CV;
using Emgu.LiteRT.Models.CV.Maui;

// In the page: a LiveCameraPreview in the layout, and the Mat it shows (kept until the next one).
LiveCameraPreview preview = new LiveCameraPreview();
Mat shown = null;

if (await Permissions.RequestAsync<Permissions.Camera>() != PermissionStatus.Granted) return;
var camera = new LiveCamera { RotationDegrees = () => LiveCamera.FrameRotation(false) ?? 0 };
camera.FrameReady = frame =>
{
    // On the camera's thread; the camera reuses its Mat, so copy it. Run a detector on another copy, off this thread.
    Mat copy = frame.Clone();
    MainThread.BeginInvokeOnMainThread(() =>
    {
        preview.Present(copy);
        shown?.Dispose();
        shown = copy;
    });
};
camera.Failed = message => MainThread.BeginInvokeOnMainThread(() => preview.ClearFrame());
await camera.StartAsync(CancellationToken.None);
// ...
await camera.StopAsync();
```

A real app keeps at most one frame waiting for the UI and drops the others, instead of queueing a copy of each.

## Note

On Android and iOS, apps on Emgu CV 4.12 should exclude `System.Drawing.Common`'s assets (`<PackageReference Include="System.Drawing.Common" Version="9.0.0" ExcludeAssets="all" />` for those targets). Emgu CV's MAUI runtime brings it through `Emgu.CV.Models`, and its Windows-only assembly makes Mono's AOT compiler fail (Android Release, iOS).

